## 🌿 4. Git Commands (Saving & Version Control)

Git saves snapshots ("commits") of your project so you never lose work and can undo mistakes easily.

| Command | What It Does | Quick Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `git status` | Checks which files have been modified, created, or deleted. | `git status` |
| `git add .` | Stages ALL modified and new files to get ready to save. | `git add .` |
| `git commit -m "message"` | Saves a snapshot of staged changes with a descriptive summary. | `git commit -m "Added v0.0.1 minion AI"` |
| `git push` | Uploads your local saved snapshots to GitHub. | `git push origin main` |
| `git pull` | Downloads the latest code changes from GitHub to your computer. | `git pull` |
| `git log --oneline` | Displays a clean 1-line list of your past saved commits. | `git log -n 5` |
| `git checkout -b <name>` | Creates and switches to a new isolated branch for safe testing. | `git checkout -b feature/pvp-mode` |

